      "math_academy_id": "q-104782",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/2a11d5b84bdf66b6406f9465ba6a8bc8ecdf364bea2dcd6902034990a74ef0a9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-3).$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14155852/assets/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-1.$",
